---

There are two ways to capture a profile. The main one lives on the dashboard at `/__profiler`: type a path and profile it. The other is a header that you can send from anywhere.

## Profile a page

Type a path. The profiler renders it several times, one after the other, and profiles only those renders. This is the main way in. Use it when you know **which page** is slow. That is almost always the case.

It does one warm-up render first, without a profile. Thus the one-time cost of module loads and cache fills lands outside the measured window, and the median stays steady.

```
/__profiler → Profile a page → /some/slow/page
```

The page renders through your real server. Thus all things it needs — a session, cookies, a `load` — run exactly as in production.

## Profile a single request

Send a request with the header `x-profile: <secret>`, and get a profile of exactly that request. The report URL comes back in the `x-profile-report` response header.

```bash
curl -sD - 'https://example.com/slow/page' -H 'x-profile: your-secret' | grep x-profile-report
```

This starts a fresh CPU profile for the one request. Thus it adds the start-up cost of the profiler to the latency of that request. Use it to catch one specific slow request, not for steady measurement.

## For agents and scripts

Each report is also available as curated JSON. This is not the raw V8 profile. It is the analyzed result: self and total per component, network attribution, memory, GC, and the same findings that the human report shows.

```bash
# an existing report
GET /__profiler/report/<id>.json

# profile a page and get the JSON back in one request (no redirect, no HTML)
GET /__profiler/page?p=/slow/page&runs=6&format=json&key=<secret>
```

`Accept: application/json` on `/record` or `/page` does the same. The payload describes itself (`schema`, `version`, and units). Thus an agent can read it directly:

```jsonc
{
  "schema": "ogygia-profiler-report",
  "summary": { "window_ms": 699, "busy_pct": 100, "verdict": "compute-bound" },
  "findings": [{ "severity": "warn", "code": "sequential-network", "message": "…" }],
  "components": [{ "name": "PrimeSieve", "file": "src/…/PrimeSieve.svelte", "self_ms": 296, "total_ms": 296 }],
  "network": { "sequential_ms": 0, "hosts": [], "calls": [] },
  "memory": { "growth_mb": 195, "gc": { "max_ms": 2.2 }, "allocators": [] }
  // budget, hot_functions, files, requests, links…
}
```

## Serverless and edge

The live dashboard wants a long-lived Node server: the V8 inspector, and memory that continues between requests. Serverless Node platforms (Lambda, Amplify) can *record*. The inspector runs in-process. But the report lives in memory, on an instance that can be gone before you open it. True edge runtimes (Workers) have no inspector. There, nothing records beyond the always-on request log.

For the serverless case, take the report with you. Each report downloads as a **self-contained JSON dump**: everything necessary to render it, with no inspector to read it back:

```bash
# a one-shot page profile, straight to a dump
GET /__profiler/page?p=/slow/page&runs=6&format=dump&key=<secret>

# or an existing report
GET /__profiler/report/<id>/dump
```

Then open **`/__profiler/view`** anywhere — your production route, or the same app on `localhost` — and upload the file. The full HTML report renders from the dump alone. The renderer is a pure function of it.

## In production

Set a secret. Without one, the UI is off and 404s.

```bash
OGYGIA_PROFILER_SECRET=some-long-random-string
```

Then open the dashboard and **log in** with the secret. The profiler sets a session cookie. Thus links inside a report operate for the remainder of the session, without the secret again. The match is timing-safe, and the key never rides in a URL, where a log, a cache, or a share would hold it. The profiler is also reachable programmatically over MCP, with `ogygia mcp` / `ogygia ai`.

## Sharper names with source maps

Production bundles rename and inline code. Thus some frames show as `(anonymous)`, or point at a chunk file. Build with server source maps, and the profiler maps each frame back to its source file and recovers the original names:

```ts
// vite.config.ts
export default defineConfig({
  build: process.env.PROFILER_SOURCEMAPS ? { sourcemap: true } : undefined,
  // ...plugins
});
```

Then build with the flag set:

```bash
PROFILER_SOURCEMAPS=1 vite build
```

The report header shows **sourcemapped** when it operated. Keep it off for normal builds. Set it on when you hunt a problem.

## Cost and safety

- **Idle cost is almost nothing.** The CPU is sampled only during a recording (about 1–3% then). Between recordings, there is no sampling.
- **Always-on work** per request: wall timing, a capped in-memory request log, and — unless you set network capture off — one `AsyncLocalStorage` wrap plus a `fetch`/`http` patch, to attribute outbound calls. Pass `{ network: false }` for the leanest path.
- **`Server-Timing` headers** show internal timings to each client. Thus they are **on in dev, off in production** by default. Pass `{ serverTiming: true }` to force them on.
- Recorded profiles stay in memory only (the last few, gzipped). They are never written to disk.

## Options

Pass an object in place of `true` to the `profiler` option of the vite plugin:

```ts
// vite.config.ts
ogygia({
  profiler: {
    secret: process.env.OGYGIA_PROFILER_SECRET, // default: the OGYGIA_PROFILER_SECRET env var
    path: '/__profiler',                        // UI base path
    sampleInterval: 500,                        // µs between CPU samples
    maxReports: 6,                              // profiles kept in memory (gzipped)
    network: true,                              // patch fetch/http to attribute calls
    serverTiming: undefined,                    // default: on in dev, off in production
    heap: true,                                 // sample heap allocations while recording
    enabled: true,                              // master switch
  },
});
```
